    report: "Market Analysis", reportIntro: "Turning market signals into management judgments, decision summaries, and clear next steps.", reportVersion: "Report version", versions: ["Current week · Live", "Last week · Archived", "2026 W31 · Archived"], liveState: "Live update", archiveState: "Official archive", liveUse: "Includes this week's latest intelligence", archiveUse: "Uses archived intelligence",
    conclusions: "Key conclusions", conclusionItems: [["Anti-Flock does not equal anti-ALPR; it is reshuffling vendors.", "NPR and Santa Barbara show agencies retaining ALPR while changing vendors or data architecture. These are recent cases, not evidence of a market-wide switch."], ["Data architecture is becoming a competitive criterion.", "Santa Barbara's proposed alternative emphasizes city ownership, department control, and no public or cross-agency search. The Verkada contract still awaits the Aug 25 hearing."], ["DFR is producing comparable operating data, but not yet a generalizable ROI case.", "Cleveland and Dallas reported scaled operating figures, while an August 22 single-agency study provides early empirical evidence. The first two are agency-reported and the study is descriptive, so none establishes market-wide causal impact."], ["Europe's near-term drone signal is more counter-drone and critical-infrastructure oriented than U.S.-style DFR.", "Germany's airport incident and Federal Police expansion plan form a threat-to-capability signal chain, while the French prison case shows a high-frequency low-cost drone threat. Neither is evidence of a completed vendor award."]],
    evolution: "How the signal is evolving", flow: [["Public backlash becomes non-renewal", "Trust and data-sharing concerns enter local decisions"], ["Agencies choose a different architecture", "ALPR stays, but closed systems and agency control gain weight"], ["Competition expands to the platform", "Devices, evidence, permissions, retention, and audit must work together"]], assessment: "Analyst assessment", assessmentText: "Buyers are asking not only who owns video data and how it enters evidence, but also how aircraft, docks, CAD/911, RTCC, and governance operate as one DFR system. Product specifications, data governance, airspace compliance, and integration delivery therefore need to form one offer.",
    decisions: "Management decision summary", decisionHeads: ["Priority", "Recommended action", "Owner", "Timing", "Next decision"], decisionRows: [["HIGH", "Build a feature matrix for shared-network versus agency-controlled architectures", "Product × Legal", "2 weeks", "Which controls become offer baseline?"], ["HIGH", "Design an ALPR replacement package covering hardware, data, permissions, and evidence integration", "Sales × Product", "3 weeks", "Launch a dedicated migration offer?"], ["MED", "Build a DFR capability matrix covering aircraft, docks, BVLOS, CAD/911, RTCC, evidence, and governance", "Product × Strategy", "4 weeks", "What to build, integrate, or partner for?"]],
